<p>Hi, yes Id be happy to elaborate! 400 words is a little restrictive.</p>
<p>The mountains were actually giants yes, the entire mountain range is the bodies of these giants who were slain in the war. And the tribes were reality benders yes but comparativley weak at it compared to other reality benders since its an aquired power and not something they have inherently like a &quot;true&quot; reality bender (if there even is a criteria.)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I was unable to include it in the main post but the war was initiated by the common &quot;betrayal of god&quot; story of many indigenous tribes which is similar somewhat to the tower of babylon in that all animals and humans and gods used to speak a common language until humanity betrays their god(s). They often (though not in all tribes) fight a war between the animals and humans in which they always lose and are forced to migrate to what ever their home is when they tell the story.</p>
<p>though god in the sense its understood in indigenous stories is that they are limitless in knowledge and in creation but they are unable to control anything they create outside of outsmarting them, since self determinism itself is something thats impossible to override, even for gods. Which is why they are forced to talk to humans occassionally.</p>
<p>As for how the giant mythos, the betrayal of god and war tie to the tribe being able to bend reality and sacrificing it,<br />
the tribe sacrificed their reality bending powers to keep the mountain they now call chief mountain from dying in the war that resulted from their betrayal of god. This tribe, who saved the chief mountain, are one of the few tribes who werent forced to migrate as the mountain sacrificed itself to kill the rest of the giants which is why the tribe sacrificed their reality bending to keep it alive, since they couldnt save it completely you can think of it as them keeping the mountain on life support through both the sacrifice of intial reality bending powers and continued rituals.</p>
<p>the mountain isnt contained by this initial sacrifice, i guess neutralized would maybe fit better but i was unsure if that would still apply if the anomoly wasnt fully neutralized and still posed a threat eventually. All the sacrifice did was keep the mountain from dying of its own sacrifice for the tribe, which was to protect them from the other giants and animals from the war.</p>
<p>The political gains part is that the mountain is still a strong ally of the small tribe and if you were a small group who lost a war to an empire and were gifted essentially a weapon of mass destruction, the literal power of a mountain to weild at your will. would you not become mad with power? would you not attempt to assert your power and claim your own piece of the pie? I thought it only seemed natural as every tribe in real life sees themselves as nations in conflict with whatever country they live in (US, Canada, mexico, etc) so if you have the nation vs nation mindset and got incredible power, asserting that power politically would naturally follow.</p>
<p>so the foundation needs to contain the mountain in one of a few ways, containing it in conjunction to the tribe, contain it without the tribes involvment at all or if nothing else killing it. This is where the political aspect comes into play again, the foundation needs to make sure the entity is contained to its area. Though they eventually realize that even if the tribe were appeased, and in the best case scenario, they werent greedy and only took a small area for their nation. The mountain itself is still autonomous so the foundation is unsure if the mountain could be kept perpetually contained by the tribes persuasion to it, as in the past humanity, and the tribes in particular, have their history of betraying their god. So they could potentially betray the mountain and cause the foundation to lose their , potentially only means of containing it<br />
so the foundation is torn between just circumventing the tribe all together and risking expending massive resources to potentially contain something but with the benefit thay if they do, itll be perpetually. as opposed to appeassing the tribe which is a known way to contain the entity, low on resources and could buy some time, but also isnt garunteed to last forever, essentially its a bandaid solution.</p>
<p>Sorry if thats alot to read, i hope you better understand how everything ties together. If you still dont understand ill try to work the story into a less complex and more focused story but i wanted to be able to explain alot of the seemingly unconnected ideas that a come to form the larger arc and not just the specific story in this specific time period, i wanted to include past and future events in some capacity.</p>
